PG.notes("l185", "<p class=\"summary\">Windows-only setup of Git for Windows so VS Code can use Git Bash as its terminal. Mac users skip it because Bash is already available.</p>\n<h3>Installing Git for Windows</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the installer from gitforwindows.org (Git SCM) and run it, accepting the admin prompt and licence.</li><li>Pick an install location; on the components screen make sure Git Bash stays ticked.</li><li>Accept the defaults on every remaining screen, including the default start-menu folder, and keep pressing Next until it finishes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying in VS Code</h3>\n<ul><li>Open Terminal &gt; New Terminal, then use the dropdown beside the + button to choose Git Bash.</li><li>A working Git Bash terminal shows a $ prompt.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The dropdown is a list of shell profiles; Git Bash only appears once Git for Windows is installed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Making Git Bash the default</h3>\n<ul><li>Open View &gt; Command Palette and run Terminal: Select Default Profile, then choose Git Bash.</li><li>Close the current terminal and open a new one; the profile label now reads bash.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Shortcut for the palette is Ctrl+Shift+P.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Set up Git Bash in VS Code (Windows)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Windows machine with VS Code installed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> New VS Code terminals open in Git Bash by default.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download and install Git for Windows with Git Bash selected.</li><li>Open a terminal in VS Code and choose Git Bash from the profile dropdown.</li><li>Run Select Default Profile from the Command Palette and pick Git Bash.</li><li>Open a fresh terminal and confirm it shows bash with a $ prompt.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Optionally run git --version in the new terminal; a version number confirms Git for Windows is installed and on the PATH.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A bash terminal opens by default in VS Code.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mac users do not need this lesson.</li><li>Existing terminals keep their old profile; open a new one after changing the default.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l186", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what a shell is, what Bash is, and why developers prefer the command line to graphical file managers.</p>\n<h3>Kernel and shell</h3>\n<ul><li>The kernel is the core of the operating system and is the program that talks directly to hardware.</li><li>A shell is the interface a person uses to reach the kernel. It comes in two kinds: graphical (e.g. Finder or Explorer) and command line interface (CLI).</li><li>Bash (Bourne Again Shell) is a command line interpreter for Unix-style systems.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where Unix lives</h3>\n<ul><li>Linux is Unix-like, most servers run Unix-like systems, and macOS is Unix-based too.</li><li>Windows comes from a different lineage (DOS), which is why Windows users install Git Bash to get the same commands.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why use the command line</h3>\n<ul><li>Control: you steer the machine more directly than through a GUI.</li><li>Speed: creating a folder is one short command (mkdir Music), and the up arrow recalls the previous command so a variation (Music2) takes seconds.</li><li>Visibility: GUIs hide config files and dot-folders; ls -a shows hidden entries, and a folder starting with a dot (mkdir .Secrets) is hidden by default.</li><li>Git and version control, taught later, are used mostly from the command line.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Create a folder, then list including hidden items</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">mkdir Music\nmkdir .Secrets\nls\nls -a</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Do not confuse the kernel (hardware-facing core) with the shell (user-facing layer).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l187", "<p class=\"summary\">Hands-on tour of ls and cd, how the prompt shows the current directory, multi-level paths, tab completion, history, and keyboard shortcuts for editing a command line.</p>\n<h3>Knowing where you are</h3>\n<ul><li>The prompt displays the current directory; a tilde (~) means the user's home directory (the folder under Users named after the account).</li><li>The directory tree starts at the disk root, then Users, then the user's folder; Finder shows the same hierarchy as the terminal.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> pwd prints the full path of the current directory, useful when the prompt only shows the last folder name (covered in the next lecture).</li></ul>\n<h3>ls: list</h3>\n<ul><li>ls prints the files and folders inside the current directory, matching what Finder would show there.</li></ul>\n<h3>cd: change directory</h3>\n<ul><li>cd Documents moves one level down into a child folder; cd Learn goes further in.</li><li>cd ~ jumps straight back to the home directory from anywhere.</li><li>A multi-level path can be given in one go with slashes: cd Documents/Learn/Languages.</li><li>cd .. moves up to the parent folder; repeat it to climb further (three times from Languages back to home).</li><li>A bare folder name only works for children of the current folder. To reach a sibling such as Music from inside Languages, go up first or give a full path.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> cd / goes to the filesystem root and cd - returns to the previous directory.</li></ul>\n<h3>Speed techniques</h3>\n<ul><li>Tab completes a partly typed name. If several names share the typed prefix, add letters until it is unambiguous (Do matches both Documents and Downloads; Dow resolves).</li><li>Up and Down arrows cycle through earlier commands; Enter re-runs the selected one.</li><li>Option/Alt-click moves the cursor to a clicked point in the command.</li><li>Ctrl+A jumps to the start of the line, Ctrl+E to the end, Ctrl+U clears the line without running it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Strictly, in Bash Ctrl+U deletes from the cursor back to the start of the line, so it clears everything only when the cursor is at the end (zsh, the macOS default, clears the whole line regardless). Ctrl+K deletes from the cursor to the end.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Navigation session</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">ls\ncd Documents\ncd Learn\ncd ~\ncd Documents/Learn/Languages\ncd ..\ncd ..\ncd ..</pre>\n<h2>The task: Navigate your own folders</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A terminal open at the home directory.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Move into a deep folder, back out, and use the shortcuts without the mouse.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run ls to see what is in the home directory.</li><li>cd into a folder one level at a time, using Tab to complete names.</li><li>Return home with cd ~.</li><li>Jump to a nested folder with a single multi-level cd path.</li><li>Climb back with repeated cd ..</li><li>Practice Up arrow, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+E and Ctrl+U on a long command.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The prompt reflects each directory you enter and leave.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>cd Music fails when Music is not inside the current folder.</li><li>Alt-click cursor placement may not work in every terminal; the keyboard shortcuts always do.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l188", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers mkdir, touch, open, pwd, rm, rm with a wildcard, and rm -r, with a strong warning about how destructive removal commands are.</p>\n<h3>Creating</h3>\n<ul><li>mkdir name creates a directory in the current location; cd into it and ls shows it is empty.</li><li>touch name.ext creates an empty file (e.g. touch Text2.txt), far quicker than making one in an editor and saving it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> touch on an existing file only updates its timestamp.</li></ul>\n<h3>Opening files (macOS)</h3>\n<ul><li>open file opens it with the default application.</li><li>open -a AppName file opens it in a chosen application via the -a flag.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Git Bash on Windows uses start file instead; the cheat sheet in the reference repo lists start.</li></ul>\n<h3>Knowing where you are</h3>\n<ul><li>The prompt shows the current folder name; pwd (print working directory) prints the full path.</li><li>Check location before any destructive command.</li></ul>\n<h3>Removing</h3>\n<ul><li>rm file deletes a file, and the deletion bypasses any trash bin.</li><li>rm * uses the * wildcard to match every file in the current directory, so it only works as intended in the right folder.</li><li>rm folder alone fails with a message that the target is a directory.</li><li>rm -r folder removes a directory and everything inside it (the -r flag means recursive).</li><li>Windows/Git Bash and Mac behave the same for these commands.</li></ul>\n<h3>Danger</h3>\n<ul><li>The GUI offers safeguards that the command line lacks.</li><li>sudo runs a command with admin rights; -f forces removal with no confirmation; combining sudo rm -rf with a root target wipes a disk irrecoverably. Never run it. The online story shown in the lecture was a hoax, but the command is real.</li><li>Further reading: the free 'Learn Enough Command Line to Be Dangerous' tutorial (covers grep, curl and more).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Full workflow</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">mkdir Angela\ncd Angela\ntouch Text2.txt\nopen Text2.txt\nopen -a TextEdit Text2.txt\npwd\nrm Text2.txt\ntouch a.txt b.txt c.txt\nrm *\ncd ..\nrm -r Angela</pre>\n<h2>The task: Make and delete files via the terminal</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Terminal in a scratch location (never your home or root for deletes).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a folder, put files in it, open one, then clean everything up.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>mkdir a new folder and cd into it.</li><li>touch several .txt files and ls to confirm.</li><li>open one in the default app, then in a chosen app with -a.</li><li>Confirm location with pwd.</li><li>Remove one file with rm, then all with rm *.</li><li>cd .. and delete the folder with rm -r.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Folder and files appear in Finder/Explorer as created, and disappear when removed.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>rm * in the wrong directory deletes the wrong files.</li><li>rm on a folder needs -r.</li><li>Never combine sudo, -rf and a root path.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l189", "<p class=\"summary\">A wellbeing tip arguing that adequate sleep improves learning, creativity and retention more than staying up late to grind.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Sleep deprivation is not a badge of seriousness: routine skills survive it, but creative thinking, new learning and making connections suffer.</li><li>Sleep helps the brain clear daytime by-products and consolidate what was learned, so cramming without sleep tends not to stick.</li><li>Practical habits mentioned: set an alarm to start winding down about an hour before bed, switch screens off, use red-tinted glasses if screens are unavoidable, read fiction, have a warm drink or small snack.</li><li>Keep a regular sleep rhythm so the next day starts fresh.</li><li>No supplement or medication replaces good sleep.</li></ul>");
